1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:04,018
NeuroCue: a synthetic aphasia patient you can run experiments on. Everything on screen is the validated engine, live.

2
00:00:04,018 --> 00:00:07,702
Choosing who to listen to: moderate severity (AQ 55), a phonological lesion.

3
00:00:07,702 --> 00:00:11,206
The patient is the Dell model of word retrieval with two lesion weights drawn from a prior fitted to real patients.

4
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:14,344
Now the patient names pictures. Nonwords are shown as the phoneme string the patient produced.

5
00:00:14,344 --> 00:00:17,365
queen → “kwiyd”  (phonological nonword)

6
00:00:17,365 --> 00:00:20,397
squirrel → “squirrel”  (correct)

7
00:00:20,397 --> 00:00:23,412
bridge → “ring”  (sound-related word)

8
00:00:23,412 --> 00:00:26,452
nurse → “pediatrician”  (semantic error)

9
00:00:26,452 --> 00:00:29,482
present → “present”  (correct)

10
00:00:29,482 --> 00:00:32,513
shovel → “shahvdahl”  (phonological nonword)

11
00:00:32,513 --> 00:00:35,526
rock → “rock”  (correct)

12
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:38,441
hay → “hhih”  (phonological nonword)

13
00:00:38,441 --> 00:00:43,275
Diagnosis: the fitter's posterior over the two lesion weights, from those eight answers alone. Bright = likely.

14
00:00:43,275 --> 00:00:48,790
Revealed: the true lesion (orange) sits inside the bright region. This is the fitter that reproduced Dell's published patients.

15
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:53,002
Same pictures, different person: AQ 50, a semantic lesion. Listen to what changes.

16
00:00:53,002 --> 00:00:55,826
queen → “emperor”  (semantic error)

17
00:00:55,826 --> 00:00:58,643
squirrel → “bear”  (semantic error)

18
00:00:58,643 --> 00:01:01,459
bridge → “widow”  (unrelated word)

19
00:01:01,459 --> 00:01:04,276
nurse → “house”  (sound-related word)

20
00:01:04,276 --> 00:01:07,093
present → “pellet”  (sound-related word)

21
00:01:07,093 --> 00:01:10,762
shovel → “phone”  (unrelated word)

22
00:01:10,762 --> 00:01:16,830
A trainer treats the patient: four sessions over ten days on 20 hard words, judged on what is still named a month later.

23
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:22,834
Words started at 27% predicted retention. After the course: hierarchy 64% at one week and 56% at one month; trained policy 66% and 58%.

24
00:01:22,834 --> 00:01:25,399
The clinician disagrees with one choice: after this error she would have given the first sound.

25
00:01:25,399 --> 00:01:27,162
Apply override: DITTO retrains the policy on this single demonstration, anchored to the original.

26
00:01:27,162 --> 00:01:34,163
After one demonstration the policy's choice changed in 10 of 160 recorded states, 5 of the 10 that followed the same kind of error. Retention after the course: trained 66%, after override 66%.

27
00:01:34,163 --> 00:01:39,172
Validated layer by layer: published model fits, 1,074 real responses (328 clinician-coded), a real therapy study, cued-naming data.

28
00:01:39,172 --> 00:01:40,176

